{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on exact post-landing corrections draft 3 a94fed569c29194ece3301cac337f08af084f9c4/tree cb3a0acd3987670a15497a3010397cf35598dd0a. I read sequenced primary 38b43f14 FIRST in full (1075 lines), reviewed all three exact artifacts, checked the package delta with its surrounding section 9 against the prior complete review, and verified the Plan 033 amendment is byte-identical. Native prepare binds the assigned review to the correct primary and implementation 59480ba2; checker owes no landing.\n\nThe two intended repairs largely work. Prior d028930e F1: the outside-order refusal now retains the pinned order journal head through which the full member is not joined, records the ordinary decision/result, answers repeats from that entry, and has an explicit verifier case plus positive/repeat/tamper witnesses in S4/S5. Prior F2: 5.1, decision 7 and packages section 9 now distinguish refused acts from delivery decisions. The post-allocation delivery refusal still uses the one prepare/fence/resolve/commit path. Two findings remain:\n\nF1 (P1), sequenced note:248, :457, :685-690 and O6 at :995: settle the superseded decision's position and replay contract. Native verified requests have three terminal decisions: applied, refused and superseded. The current native judge writes a deciding superseded entry/result for an older relate revision before running the handler; source-ordered tell suppression does the same (Main 1da3f4ca, derive/delivery.ts:188-212). O6 proposes allocating after the relate revision check, explicitly saying a superseded update takes no position. If that proposal is followed for a joined member's sequenced relate handler, the older update must write its native superseded deciding entry without a position. But the new 5.8 rule permits only refused order-left/not-a-member without a position and rejects every other such entry. The genuine superseded entry cannot pass it. Choosing allocation instead is not yet specified either: 5.2 and step 6 currently enumerate applied judgments and refused deliveries for preparing, and O6 would have to change.\n\nPlease choose and state this composition before S3-S5 implement the generic handler form: either an explicitly checked kernel superseded decision outside allocation, with the source/revision or frontier evidence, deciding-entry/result/repeat rules and verifier exception; or a positioned superseded judgment through prepare/resolve/commit, with its costs and O6 aligned; or an explicit validator restriction on the unsupported composition. Cover the existing relate revision case and the source-ordered tell case consistently. Add the native/replay witness and a tamper case, retaining rejection of an ordinary applied entry without position. O6 is labelled an open proposal, not an adopted runtime rule; the finding is that its proposed no-position path conflicts with the newly exhaustive replay rule and the alternative is also unspecified. This gap predates draft 3 and was missed in my earlier pass. It affects sequencing composition in S3-S5.\n\nF2 (P2), sequenced note:708-709: qualify the old blanket no-position sentence at the end of 5.8. It still says every sequenced input without position is a mismatch, immediately after the new bullet defines a genuine outside-order exception. Keep the explicit exception and make this sentence the default rule subject to the enumerated, independently checked cases. This is the remaining current-rule summary residue; the three summaries named by prior F2 are corrected.\n\nArchitecture: changes required for the remaining terminal-decision coverage and the contradictory summary. The retained order-head proof resolves the specific leave/not-a-member refusal problem, and reusing prepare/commit for post-allocation refusal remains the right simplification.\n\nSecurity: the new refusal exception requires journal evidence, not merely a claimed reason, preserving the source verification and duplicate/result contract. Replay must also verify the chosen superseded path rather than accepting or rejecting it solely because its handler declares sequenced. Existing pinned group identity, full incarnation, finality, effective-fact and page-domain requirements remain in force. Platform scopes remain excluded by 5.10/O3; no native platform sequencing composition is approved here.\n\nSimplification: use one explicit table of terminal request decisions and whether they allocate, then align turn, entry, replay and costs. Keep prepared judgment/recovery shared for decisions that allocate, and reserve narrowly checked exceptions for decisions that do not. No additional release protocol is needed.\n\nThe packages note retains request-only late/spacing and advisory rejection. The separate S1 native reserved/item-bound restrictions adopted in planner report 56398945 remain separate implementation obligations. Plan 033 still preserves the archive binding, reviewed hosting policy/configuration, actual destination/token, A3 wiring, disclosure, G5 -\u003e archive -\u003e G4 order, and A2/A3 fixture-only cutoff.\n\nValidation: /private/tmp/checker-post-landing-docs-draft3-62veeimk/validation.json verifies all 2249 Git leaves/modes/blob IDs, with 2246 prior leaves unchanged against baseline 165d5a86/tree 1b69aa4787f8a64fca1bc299567c886527bdc27f. Only the three documentation paths differ and whitespace passes. Source/tests are unchanged, so no gate or suite was repeated under docs/testing.md. design-traces.json contains the retained-proof refusal and superseded/replay consistency traces as manual design evidence; the sequenced runtime is not implemented or executed. No source/ref mutation, install, push or deployment was performed by checker.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:38b43f14db2fa4ab5c5bf90c2f24368bb27405bb","binding":"assigned","head":"a94fed569c29194ece3301cac337f08af084f9c4","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2